\section{Predicates Describe Relations}

A build-plan checker needs to answer questions in several directions: which artifacts can be produced from a source format, which source formats can produce a requested artifact, and whether a particular combination is possible. A function with a fixed input and output can answer one direction directly. A relation contains tuples without designating any of their positions as inputs. A goal can fill some positions with constants and leave the rest for the logic engine to find. Whether the engine can \emph{efficiently enumerate} the results in a given direction is a separate question.

Kowalski's procedural reading of predicate logic interprets a rule head as a callable goal and its body as the goals needed to establish it \cite{kowalski1974predicate}. Van Emden and Kowalski's 1976 paper, \emph{The Semantics of Predicate Logic as a Programming Language}, relates that reading to a model-theoretic one. In the definite-clause fragment, a rule says that whenever all the body atoms hold, the head holds. A fact is a clause with an empty body. Variables are local to a clause and universally quantified there. The rule is not a biconditional: it gives a sufficient condition, not permission to deduce the body from the head.

\section{One Small Program, Several Queries}

Here is an executable Prolog program. The first relation names a front end and the intermediate representation it emits; the second names the artifact available from each representation.

\begin{verbatim}
front(src, tree).
front(src, ssa).
front(dsl, tree).

emit(tree, report).
emit(ssa, obj).
emit(ssa, report).

plan(Src, Out) :-
    front(Src, Mid),
    emit(Mid, Out).
\end{verbatim}

The shared variable \texttt{Mid} means the two body atoms must agree on the intermediate representation. The query \verb|?- plan(src, obj).| succeeds, while \verb|?- plan(dsl, obj).| finitely fails on this database. The query \verb|?- plan(S, obj).| returns \verb|S = src|. The query \verb|?- plan(src, O).| can return \texttt{report}, \texttt{obj}, and \texttt{report} again, in that order, when the caller requests all answers from an ordinary Prolog engine. Two proofs for one tuple do not make two copies of it in the relation.

An answer is a substitution projected onto the variables of the original goal. A ground query has no variables to report, so success gives the empty substitution; that is different from returning no answer. An answer need not be ground in a general logic program: a fact \verb|same(X, X).| queried as \verb|same(A, B)| can report that \texttt{A} and \texttt{B} must be the same without giving either a constant value. The choice of printed representative for their shared variable is not significant. Nils M.\ Holm's \emph{Logic Programming in Scheme} makes the analogous distinction between a goal, its stream of results, and the reification of an answer for display.

