\section{Walkthrough: From Compatible Rows to Answers}

For the finite program above, the following evaluator pseudocode computes \texttt{plan} by a join followed by projection. It describes this rule on these complete ground inputs, not a replacement for Prolog resolution on arbitrary clauses.

\begin{verbatim}
result := empty set
for (source, middle) in front:
    for (key, output) in emit:
        if key == middle:               # enforce the shared variable
            result.add((source, output)) # discard the witness column
return result
\end{verbatim}

After each outer iteration, the invariant is that \texttt{result} contains exactly the projections of matching row pairs for the \texttt{front} rows processed so far. The \texttt{(src,tree)} row adds \texttt{(src,report)}. The \texttt{(src,ssa)} row adds \texttt{(src,obj)} and proposes \texttt{(src,report)} again; the set stays unchanged on that second insertion. The \texttt{(dsl,tree)} row adds \texttt{(dsl,report)}. A nonmatching pair such as \texttt{(dsl,tree)} with \texttt{(ssa,obj)} yields nothing. \Cref{fig:visual-338} isolates the two witnesses that produce the duplicate proof.

