\section{Keep Meaning Separate from the Enumeration Contract}

Luger and Stubblefield's \emph{AI Algorithms, Data Structures, and Idioms in Prolog, Lisp, and Java}, in its Prolog introduction, follows answers by retrying alternatives on backtracking. In the pure definite-clause model, reordering the two body atoms does not change the conjunction or the resulting relation. As Prolog code, the reordering changes which goal gets called with unbound arguments, which alternatives are tried first, and how much work is done. With recursion a reorder may even decide whether the caller sees an answer at all. Cuts and input/output effects are further changes to the model, not harmless ways of spelling conjunction.

Specify what a client needs before choosing an evaluator: a yes/no test, the first witness, the set of all answers, or every proof. For the small complete database above, a failed ground lookup means the tuple is outside the least model of the supplied rules and facts. It is not a universal assertion that the compiler could never gain a new rule producing that artifact. In tests, check the set of expected tuples separately from assumptions about answer order, proof multiplicity, and termination.

At a compiler boundary, make that choice part of the pass interface: a planner that chooses one backend may consume a witness, whereas a cache of feasible source--artifact pairs should consume a deduplicated relation. Do not let an engine's repeated proof output silently become repeated compilation work.
